{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2955","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2955","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2955\",\"parent_revision\":\"b08ec1dc428a7446f91c3ca70a6192e435d0f7c3\",\"root_revision\":\"38eb8f829178a97f9b26527703a6bb39c26709afd648b029350d3ceeb06d6fa5\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Chore\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-17\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nRun a second, continuous thread across this and future sessions: a working register of every concern, workaround, gotcha, and correction that surfaces from using the development-harness (dh) plugin. A month of accumulated friction with it has gone unrecorded \u2014 capture it in the moment, not from end-of-session memory.\\n\\n## What triggers an entry\\n\\nLog the instant one of these four things happens, mid-task:\\n\\n- **Concern** \u2014 dh behavior that reads as risky, fragile, or wrong, even if it doesn't block the current task.\\n- **Workaround** \u2014 routed around a dh limitation to get the primary task done.\\n- **Gotcha** \u2014 dh did something surprising or non-obvious that cost time to diagnose.\\n- **Correction** \u2014 the user redirected how the agent used dh (a fix to approach, not to what was built).\\n\\n## Entry format\\n\\nAppend via `backlog_groom(item, section=\\\"Working Register\\\", append=True, content=...)`, one call per occurrence:\\n\\n```\\n[category] <one-line summary>\\nWhere: <command / tool / skill invoked>\\nWhat happened: <observed behavior>\\nWhy it matters: <impact or risk>\\n```\\n\\n## End of session\\n\\nCount the session's new entries. At 3+, ask the user whether to spin any into their own backlog items now (`/dh:work-backlog-item create`) or leave them batched for a later grooming pass. Leave this register item open regardless \u2014 it is cumulative, not a single-session task, and closes only when the user decides the log itself should be groomed into standalone items and retired.\\n\\nThis item is BEHAVIORAL/PROCESS: the procedural description above is the requirement specification for the agent's ongoing logging behavior, not implementation instructions to strip.\\n\\n## Working Register\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:43:40.844852Z</sub>\\n\\n[concern] `backlog_list(from_github=true)` on a full duplicate-check triggers a full-repo sync, not a scoped query\\nWhere: `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_list` with `from_github=true` during #2953 grooming/register setup duplicate-check\\nWhat happened: Called to force-refresh the local cache before a title-substring duplicate check. The backend reported 387 open items and the call ran 5+ minutes without completing, getting moved to a background MCP task (`kkra0czcb`) by the harness. No progress/streaming signal during the wait.\\nWhy it matters: There's no apparent way to scope a \\\"refresh\\\" to just the items relevant to a search \u2014 any `from_github=true` call appears to pull the entire backend, making a routine duplicate-check before item creation expensive and slow if you actually need fresh data. In practice this forces reliance on the (possibly stale/incomplete) local cache for time-sensitive duplicate checks, which risks false negatives (missing a real duplicate) on a repo with 387 open items and only ~16 cached.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:48:58.000709Z</sub>\\n\\n[concern] `backlog_groom` reports the identical \\\"29 pending mutation(s)\\\" count across consecutive, unrelated writes\\nWhere: `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom` on #2953 \u2014 two separate calls (RT-ICA section write, then Working Register append) each returned \\\"Reconciled #2953: 1 provider patch(es), 29 pending mutation(s), 0 failure(s).\\\"\\nWhat happened: The pending-mutation count did not change between two consecutive successful writes to two different items (#2953 and #2955), despite each call reporting 0 failures and a successful reconcile.\\nWhy it matters: If pending mutations were actually being drained, the count should trend toward 0 across writes, or at least vary. An unchanging count after \\\"successful\\\" reconciliation suggests either (a) these are stuck/unprocessable queued mutations that reconcile is silently failing to clear, or (b) the reported number isn't what it appears to be (e.g. a static/global count unrelated to this item). Either way it's opaque \u2014 there's no tool surfaced yet to inspect what the 29 pending mutations actually are.\\n</div>\\n\\n## End Of Session\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nCount the session's new entries. At 3+, ask the user whether to spin any into their own backlog items now (`/dh:work-backlog-item create`) or leave them batched for a later grooming pass. Leave this register item open regardless \u2014 it is cumulative, not a single-session task, and closes only when the user decides the log itself should be groomed into standalone items and retired.\\n\\nThis item is BEHAVIORAL/PROCESS: the procedural description above is the requirement specification for the agent's ongoing logging behavior, not implementation instructions to strip.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the project infrastructure**, I want to **dh plugin working register: ongoing log of concerns, workarounds, gotchas, and corrections** so that **the project infrastructure stays healthy**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## What Triggers An Entry\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nLog the instant one of these four things happens, mid-task:\\n\\n- **Concern** \u2014 dh behavior that reads as risky, fragile, or wrong, even if it doesn't block the current task.\\n- **Workaround** \u2014 routed around a dh limitation to get the primary task done.\\n- **Gotcha** \u2014 dh did something surprising or non-obvious that cost time to diagnose.\\n- **Correction** \u2014 the user redirected how the agent used dh (a fix to approach, not to what was built).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Entry Format\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAppend via `backlog_groom(item, section=\\\"Working Register\\\", append=True, content=...)`, one call per occurrence:\\n\\n```\\n[category] <one-line summary>\\nWhere: <command / tool / skill invoked>\\nWhat happened: <observed behavior>\\nWhy it matters: <impact or risk>\\n```\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 direct user instruction to run a continuous, cross-session working register alongside primary task work\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-17\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Working Register\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:43:40.844852Z</sub>\\n\\n[concern] `backlog_list(from_github=true)` on a full duplicate-check triggers a full-repo sync, not a scoped query\\nWhere: `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_list` with `from_github=true` during #2953 grooming/register setup duplicate-check\\nWhat happened: Called to force-refresh the local cache before a title-substring duplicate check. The backend reported 387 open items and the call ran 5+ minutes without completing, getting moved to a background MCP task (`kkra0czcb`) by the harness. No progress/streaming signal during the wait.\\nWhy it matters: There's no apparent way to scope a \\\"refresh\\\" to just the items relevant to a search \u2014 any `from_github=true` call appears to pull the entire backend, making a routine duplicate-check before item creation expensive and slow if you actually need fresh data. In practice this forces reliance on the (possibly stale/incomplete) local cache for time-sensitive duplicate checks, which risks false negatives (missing a real duplicate) on a repo with 387 open items and only ~16 cached.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"19adf29e98cee306844e89